<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ngo dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">939 CE – 965 CE · capital Co Loa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first independent Vietnamese dynasty after a thousand years of Chinese rule, founded by Ngo Quyen after his victory at Bach Dang in 938.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-quyen">Ngo Quyen</a>, son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-dinh-nghe">Duong Dinh Nghe</a>, defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-han">Southern Han</a> navy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bach-dang-river">Bach Dang</a> river by planting iron-tipped stakes in the riverbed and luring the fleet onto them at low tide. In 939 he declared himself king and set his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/co-loa-citadel">Co Loa</a>, the ancient citadel of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/au-lac">Au Lac</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-quyen">Ngo Quyen</a> established a court with officials and rituals on the Chinese model, but his authority depended on alliances with regional strongmen. After his death in 944 his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duong-tam-kha">Duong Tam Kha</a> seized power, until Ngo Quyen's sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-xuong-ngap">Ngo Xuong Ngap</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-xuong-van">Ngo Xuong Van</a> restored the family and ruled jointly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The dynasty lost control as local warlords asserted themselves, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-xuong-van">Ngo Xuong Van's</a> death in 965 the country broke into the period of the Twelve Warlords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Dinh Bo Linh</a> reunified it in 968, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dinh-dynasty">Dinh dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bach-dang-river">Bach Dang</a> became a national symbol of resistance.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ngo-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ngo dynasty" width="320" height="394"><figcaption>Image: TRMC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_T%C4%A9nh_H%E1%BA%A3i_qu%C3%A2n_and_early_years_of_Ng%C3%B4_dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>939 CE – 965 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Co Loa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ngo Quyen, Ngo Xuong Van</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nhà Ngô</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=952&amp;focus=ngo-dynasty" aria-label="Ngo dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7526 1397 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ngo dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7846 1653L7856 1684"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7856 1684h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=952&amp;focus=ngo-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
